H2: Known Faults ƒ?" Jeep Wrangler
H3: Petrol Stalling and Engine Noises (JK)
P: MONITOR ¶ú Any miles ¶ú Get a quote
P: RAC Drive gathered owner reports on the JK (2007 - 2018). The most serious was a petrol model that stalled for no obvious reason at 55mph. That car also had a ticking noise from the engine, and another owner reported engine knocking. Each is one ownerƒ?Ts report, not a survey count, but ask the seller whether the engine has ever stalled or made odd noises.
H3: 3.8-litre V6 Oil Use (JK)
P: CHECK ¶ú Any miles ¶ú Get a quote
P: Auto Express says the 3.8-litre engine tends to use a lot of oil, and that if the level isnƒ?Tt topped up regularly the V6 motor will seize. Its review covers the 2007 to 2018 car. Ask how the previous owner kept the oil level and check the service history.
H3: ABS and Traction Warning Lights, Uconnect Screen Problems (JK)
P: MONITOR ¶ú Any miles ¶ú Get a quote
P: One owner in RAC Driveƒ?Ts JK review highlighted ABS and traction dash warning lights that he said come on constantly. The same review notes some issues with the Uconnect infotainment screen, covering a glitchy audio set-up and sat nav problems. Start the car and watch the dashboard before you drive it.
H3: Automatic Gearbox and Differential Complaints (JK)
P: MONITOR ¶ú Any miles ¶ú Get a quote
P: RAC Drive heard from one JK owner whose automatic transmission wouldnƒ?Tt shift out of first gear. Its review of the 2007 to 2017 car also says intrusive transmission whine can mean a new differential. On a test drive, listen for whine and make sure the automatic changes up through the gears.
H3: Leaks Through the Hard-Top, Footwell and Doors (JK)
P: MONITOR ¶ú Any miles ¶ú Get a quote
P: Auto Express says the three-door JK is a convertible with a lift-off hard-top that is prone to leaks, so check for water in the car and for rust. A Carbuyer owner of a 2014 diesel two-door Overland had water leaking in and filling the driverƒ?Ts footwell, and cured it by replacing the carpets with rubber matting and pulling the drain plugs. A Parkers owner of a 2014 CRD Overland four-door automatic reports leaking on the front doors, which an extra seal around the frame solved.
H3: Tired Dampers, Ball Joints, Gaiters and Oil Seals (JK)
P: MONITOR ¶ú Any miles ¶ú Get a quote
P: Auto Express says Wranglers are more likely to be used for off-roading than most cars, so check any potential buy for tired dampers and worn ball joints. It adds that the driveshaftƒ?Ts rubber gaiters can tear easily, which lets muck in. RAC Driveƒ?Ts advice is to check the steering for play and also check all oil seals.
H3: Fuel Tank Leak and Seat Belt Failure (JK Owner Reports)
P: CHECK ¶ú Any miles ¶ú Get a quote
P: Two single reports in RAC Driveƒ?Ts JK owner feedback stand out. One owner had a leaking fuel tank, and on another car the passenger seat belt broke. Look under the car for fuel staining and test every seat belt.
H3: Four-Cylinder and Straight-Six Engine Faults (TJ)
P: MONITOR ¶ú Any miles ¶ú Get a quote
P: Autocarƒ?Ts buying guide to the TJ (1996 to 2006) says that because it has to work harder, the 122bhp 2.5-litre four-cylinder petrol engine can be more troublesome. It also says the 4.0-litre is prone to water pump issues, so check for leaks, and that pre-2000 4.0-litre engines can suffer a cracked exhaust manifold.
H3: Transfer Box and Rear Differential Faults (TJ)
P: MONITOR ¶ú Any miles ¶ú Get a quote
P: Autocar says the TJƒ?Ts transfer box can seize up if four-wheel drive isnƒ?Tt regularly selected, so make sure it engages smoothly. It also says the Belleville spring washer that tensions the clutch plates in the rear differential can crack, making the differential noisy.
H3: Chassis Rot and Vague Steering (TJ)
P: CHECK ¶ú Any miles ¶ú Get a quote
P: Autocar warns that some parallel imports arenƒ?Tt as well rust-proofed as official UK cars and their chassis can rot. On steering, it says the TJ was hardly crisp out of the box, but if it is seriously vague, suspect worn ball joints.
H3: Short Battery Life and Electrical Faults (JL)
P: MONITOR ¶ú Any miles ¶ú Get a quote
P: In its buyerƒ?Ts guide to the 2018 to 2026 car, Auto Express says batteries donƒ?Tt seem to last very long, which can lead to the carƒ?Ts electrics doing weird things or not working at all. It adds that loose fuses can also result in some electrical functions working only intermittently. Check every switch and screen function when you view the car.
H3: Windscreen Damage, Sun Visors and Paint (JL)
P: MINOR ¶ú Any miles ¶ú Get a quote
P: Auto Express says JL windscreens get damaged easily, partly because theyƒ?Tre so upright. It also reports that owner forums talk about sun visors breaking, with stronger ones available, and poor paint adhesion leading to bubbling. Inspect the glass and paintwork closely.
H2: What to check when buying
P: RAC Driveƒ?Ts advice for the JL (2018 - 2022) is to pay attention on the drive to how shifting feels and to note any unstable handling, jerks or vibrations that could indicate a death wobble in the future. It also says to listen for unexpected idling upon warm-up, stuttering during idling and loud ticking.
P: RAC Drive adds that a spotty maintenance history could set you up for early part failure. Ask for every service record, and run the registration through our recall check to see whether a DVSA safety recall is still open.
P: Whichever generation you are looking at, take it up to road speed, switch every electrical function on and off, and then look underneath, on a ramp if you can.
H2: What reviews and surveys say about reliability
P: Carbuyerƒ?Ts review of the 2018 onwards Wrangler says Jeep didnƒ?Tt feature in its 2023 Driver Power customer service satisfaction survey, and that parent company Fiat came a disappointing place near the bottom. Auto Express says the Wrangler is too niche for its Driver Power surveys, but that reviews on sister site Carbuyer are almost universally glowing.
P: RAC Driveƒ?Ts survey of JL owners found most seemed pretty happy with their cars, though inevitably some buyers had issues. Parkers says Jeep has slightly wavering reliability in general, but Wranglers donƒ?Tt seem to be too affected by major issues.
H2: Annoyances rather than faults
P: Some complaints are about the way the car is built rather than something that breaks. A Carbuyer owner of a 2012 four-door diesel says the doors donƒ?Tt stay open, which is a pain, but thereƒ?Ts a kit to modify it. Another owner, of a 2008 diesel manual Sport Renegade Pack, reports a tendency for the back end to slip out under hard acceleration, and says the soft top takes longer than pitching a tent.
